---
// src/pages/ddos-protection.astro
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout 
  title="DDoS Protection & Detection Sourcing | InfraHub"
  description="Compare always-on cloud protection, on-demand BGP diversion, FastNetMon detection automation, and StormWall DDoS protection architectures."
  canonicalPath="/ddos-protection"
>
  <div class="section section-hero network-overlay">
    <div class="container service-container">
      <div class="service-header">
        <div class="header-badge-row">
          <span class="pill-badge badge-blue">Security & Network Defense</span>
          <span class="pill-badge badge-teal">Partner Delivered</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="service-title">Multi-Layer DDoS Protection & Detection</h1>
        <p class="service-lead">
          Evaluate network-layer detection, BGP-based traffic diversion, external cloud scrubbing, and web-application protection as separate parts of a DDoS architecture. InfraHub helps clarify which controls fit the traffic profile, network design, and operational model before introducing a specialist provider.
        </p>
        <div class="service-hero-actions">
          <a href="/lets-talk?service=ddos-protection" class="btn btn-accent btn-lg">Request DDoS Evaluation</a>
          <a href="#architectures" class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg">Compare Architectures</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="content-section" id="architectures">
        <span class="section-sub-tag">Mitigation Models</span>
        <h2>Common DDoS Defense Architectures</h2>
        <div class="architecture-grid">
          <div class="arch-card">
            <span class="arch-pill">Cloud Scrubbing</span>
            <h3>Always-On Diversion</h3>
            <p>Traffic is continuously routed through a protection provider so malicious traffic can be filtered before clean traffic is returned to the protected network or application.</p>
            <ul class="arch-points">
              <li><strong>Consider:</strong> scrubbing location, return path, latency, and capacity model</li>
              <li><strong>Trade-off:</strong> protection is continuously in path rather than activated only during an incident</li>
              <li><strong>Fits:</strong> services where continuous upstream protection is preferred</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="arch-card">
            <span class="arch-pill">On-Demand</span>
            <h3>BGP Route Diversion</h3>
            <p>Traffic follows its normal path until an incident triggers route changes that divert affected prefixes through external filtering infrastructure.</p>
            <ul class="arch-points">
              <li><strong>Consider:</strong> detection trigger, routing convergence, tunnel or cross-connect return path</li>
              <li><strong>Trade-off:</strong> the mitigation path must be activated and routing changes must propagate</li>
              <li><strong>Fits:</strong> networks that want normal routing outside attack periods</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="arch-card">
            <span class="arch-pill">Flow Telemetry</span>
            <h3>Detection & Routing Automation</h3>
            <p>Tools such as FastNetMon analyze live network telemetry and can trigger operator-defined FlowSpec, RTBH, or external scrubbing-diversion actions.</p>
            <ul class="arch-points">
              <li><strong>Consider:</strong> telemetry sources, thresholds, router support, and escalation policy</li>
              <li><strong>Trade-off:</strong> detection and routing automation do not replace payload-cleaning capacity when scrubbing is required</li>
              <li><strong>Fits:</strong> ISPs, hosting platforms, cloud operators, and other networks controlling their own routing policy</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="content-section">
        <span class="section-sub-tag">Decision Framework</span>
        <h2>Technical Decision Factors</h2>
        <div class="factors-table-wrap">
          <table class="factors-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Criteria</th>
                <th>Consideration</th>
                <th>Why It Matters</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Scrubbing Capacity</strong></td>
                <td>Provider capacity, location, and protected-traffic model</td>
                <td>The protection path needs enough headroom for the attack profile without turning the buyer's own internet edge into the bottleneck.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Return Traffic Path</strong></td>
                <td>GRE/IPIP tunnel, IX, direct connection, or other provider handoff</td>
                <td>Return-path design affects routing, MTU planning, operational complexity, and where clean traffic rejoins the network.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>BGP FlowSpec / RTBH</strong></td>
                <td>Router compatibility and operator-defined policy</td>
                <td>Routing-based controls can distribute targeted filters or blackhole routes without requiring every mitigation action to be performed manually.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Application-Layer Protection</strong></td>
                <td>Web proxying, WAF, bot controls, and TLS handling</td>
                <td>Network-layer mitigation and web-application protection solve different problems and may need to be combined for internet-facing applications.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="content-section">
        <div class="infrahub-role-card">
          <span class="pill-badge badge-teal">Specialist Ecosystem</span>
          <h2>FastNetMon & StormWall</h2>
          <p>
            These partners address different layers of the DDoS problem. InfraHub can help determine whether the requirement calls for detection automation, external mitigation capacity, web protection, or a combination.
          </p>
          <div class="role-points-grid">
            <div class="role-point">
              <h3><a href="/partners/fastnetmon">FastNetMon</a></h3>
              <p>Network-layer DDoS detection and mitigation control plane supporting operator-defined BGP FlowSpec, RTBH, and external scrubbing-diversion workflows.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="role-point">
              <h3><a href="/partners/stormwall">StormWall</a></h3>
              <p>Cloud-based DDoS protection for networks, TCP/UDP services, and web applications, with BGP-based network protection plus WAF and antibot options.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="role-point">
              <h3>Combined Architecture</h3>
              <p>A buyer may pair local or network-owned detection policy with external mitigation capacity when that separation fits its routing, operating, and commercial requirements.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="service-footer-cta">
        <h2>Review Your DDoS Architecture</h2>
        <p>Start with traffic profile, routing control, protected services, and the operational response you need.</p>
        <a href="/lets-talk?service=ddos-protection" class="btn btn-accent btn-lg">Discuss DDoS Protection</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .service-container { max-width: 1040px; }
  .service-header { text-align: left; max-width: 860px; margin: 0 0 64px; padding-bottom: 24px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color); }
  .header-badge-row { display: flex; justify-content: flex-start; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 16px; }
  .service-title { font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4vw, 3.4rem); font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.15; margin-bottom: 20px; }
  .service-lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.65; margin-bottom: 32px; }
  .service-hero-actions { display: flex; justify-content: flex-start; gap: 14px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .content-section { margin-bottom: 72px; }
  .section-sub-tag { display: block; font-family: var(--font-heading); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--accent-blue); margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .content-section h2 { font-size: 1.85rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 18px; }

  .architecture-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 24px; }
  @media (min-width: 768px) { .architecture-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }
  .arch-card { background-color: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 28px; box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm); display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .arch-pill { display: inline-block; font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--accent-teal); margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .arch-card h3 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .arch-card p { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.5; margin-bottom: 16px; }
  .arch-points { list-style: disc; padding-left: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-secondary); }

  .factors-table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; background-color: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); }
  .factors-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .factors-table th, .factors-table td { padding: 16px 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color); }
  .factors-table th { background-color: var(--bg-secondary); font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .factors-table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

  .infrahub-role-card { background-color: var(--bg-dark); color: #ffffff; border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 44px 36px; }
  .infrahub-role-card h2 { color: #ffffff; margin: 12px 0 16px; }
  .infrahub-role-card > p { color: var(--text-on-dark-muted); font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 32px; }
  .role-points-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 24px; }
  @media (min-width: 768px) { .role-points-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }
  .role-point { background-color: transparent; border: none; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12); border-radius: 0; padding: 18px 0 0; }
  .role-point h3 { color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .role-point h3 a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
  .role-point h3 a:hover { color: #93c5fd; }
  .role-point p { color: var(--text-on-dark-muted); font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  .service-footer-cta { padding: 40px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; text-align: left; gap: 16px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color); }
</style>
